From 68f35c4887954330184b603b1629a2b2788a80bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <services.claude@accounts.ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 20:45:01 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] patch 0283 -- hold the replayed input mask between events
 (THE TAS desync)

The .TAS format coalesces: an event is written only when the input mask
CHANGES. A key held from tick 158 to tick 183 is therefore two events, not
25. Replay applied a mask ONLY on ticks carrying an event and returned,
leaving inputs[] holding whatever input_poll() had just written -- nothing,
since no human is at the keyboard during playback. Every recorded press
survived exactly one tick out of however many it was really held.

Sustained movement therefore never happened: the player twitched for a
single tick per event and stayed put, which is the "jumps around in the same
place" the operator reported, and the long-standing "clean replay is not
sync proof, it desyncs into a coherent but WRONG scene" note in our own
findings. That note described this bug without identifying it.

Caught by diffing a record trace against its replay trace (patch 0282
instrumentation) from one 90-second round trip on g2k:

  record: t=175 mode=1 map=20 px=71845 py=73728 mask=0x2
  replay: t=175 mode=1 map=20 px=66065 py=73728 mask=0x0

with the reel itself carrying `t=158 mask=0x2` and `t=183 mask=0x0` -- so
RIGHT was unambiguously held across tick 175 and replay had already dropped
it. px frozen at the pre-input value is the proof.

Fix: track the held mask, update it as events are consumed, and apply it on
EVERY tick. This is in 0135's original replay logic, so TAS playback has
been wrong for every reel that ever held a key longer than one tick -- i.e.
all of them. 0280/0281 (accumulator inflation) were real and worth keeping,
but they were not this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/tas.cpp | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/tas.cpp b/src/tas.cpp
index b9c5766b..31fff0c4 100644
--- a/src/tas.cpp
+++ b/src/tas.cpp
@@ -56,6 +56,19 @@ static const uint32_t TAS_STREAM_MODE_N_EVENTS = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
 static FILE *_record_fp = nullptr;
 static FILE *_replay_fp = nullptr;
 
+// patch 0283: the input mask currently HELD during replay.
+//
+// The file format coalesces -- an event is written only when the mask
+// CHANGES -- so a key held from tick 158 to tick 183 is two events, not 25.
+// Replay must therefore apply the last-seen mask on EVERY tick, not only on
+// ticks that carry an event. Applying it only on event ticks left inputs[]
+// holding whatever input_poll() had just written (nothing, since no human is
+// at the keyboard), so every recorded press survived exactly ONE tick out of
+// however many it was really held. Sustained movement never happened and the
+// run diverged immediately -- the long-standing "replays into a coherent but
+// wrong scene" symptom.
+static uint32_t _replay_held_mask = 0;
+
 // Last-recorded input mask. Used to coalesce: we only write an event
 // when the mask CHANGES, not on every tick. Initialized to a sentinel
 // that can't equal any real mask so the first tick's mask is always
@@ -367,12 +380,12 @@ void apply_input(uint32_t tick_id, bool *inputs, int n_inputs)
     if (!_record_fp && !_replay_fp) return;
 
     if (_replay_fp) {
-        // Apply every event whose tick is <= tick_id. The <= (not ==)
-        // tolerates the tick-skipping case per sec. E.3 of the design
-        // (cooperative-scheduler starvation can skip a tick; we fire
-        // the event late rather than abort).
+        // Consume every event whose tick is <= tick_id, updating the HELD
+        // mask. The <= (not ==) tolerates the tick-skipping case per sec. E.3
+        // of the design (cooperative-scheduler starvation can skip a tick; we
+        // fire the event late rather than abort).
         while (_replay_pending_valid && _replay_pending_tick <= tick_id) {
-            _inputs_from_mask(_replay_pending_mask, inputs, n_inputs);
+            _replay_held_mask = _replay_pending_mask;
             if (_replay_pending_tick < tick_id) {
                 // Late-fire diagnostic. Useful for catching divergence
                 // in real-HW iters; intentionally LOG_DEBUG (silent in
@@ -384,6 +397,9 @@ void apply_input(uint32_t tick_id, bool *inputs, int n_inputs)
             }
             _prefetch_next_event();
         }
+        // patch 0283: apply the held mask on EVERY tick, event or not. This
+        // is what makes a held key stay held for its recorded duration.
+        _inputs_from_mask(_replay_held_mask, inputs, n_inputs);
         return;
     }
 
